  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon, water vapor satellite imagery along with upper \nair analysis showed an upper level trough across the Four Corners \nregion. An upper level trough extended from the Hudson Bay region \nsouthwest across the western Great Lakes into the mid MS River \nValley. An upper trough was located across Alberta, Canada.\n\nthe 18Z surface map showed a ridge of high pressure extending from \neastern SD, southward across eastern KS into OK. Temperatures ranged \nfrom the mid to upper 30s along the NE border, to the lower 40s \nalong and south of I-70.\n\nToday through Thursday (Thanksgiving):\n\nThe H5 trough across the Four Corners region will dig east-southeast \nacross southern OK and central TX. There maybe enough assent ahead of \nthe H5 trough along with upslope flow for some light snow across \neastern CO and far western KS. The better assent will remain \nwell southwest of the CWA. During the day on Tuesday the H5 Trough \nacross Alberta, Canada will dig southeast into the northern Plains. \nThe ECMWF, GFS, and REFS show the northern stream H5 wave across the \nnorthern Plains digging southeast and phasing with the southern \nstream H5 trough shifting east into AR. The phasing of the two H5 \ntroughs will form a deeper upper low across far eastern KS and \nwestern MO, this upper low will dig southeast into AR Wednesday \nnight, then lift northeast into the OH River Valley on Thursday \n(Thanksgiving). As the upper low deepens along the KS/MO border \nWednesday morning, there will be some weak frontogenetical forcing on\nthe west side of the upper low, so there could be enough ascent for \nsome snow flurries Wednesday morning across northeast KS. However, \nisentropic downglide in the lower levels of the atmosphere will \nprevent any light snow from developing. The stronger upper ascent \nwill develop east of the CWA across MO into the southern Great Lakes \nWednesday night into Thursday night. Thursday may be breezy as \nsurface gradient develops between a deepening surface low along the \nmid MS River Valley and higher surface pressure across western KS. \nNorthwest winds of 15 to 20 MPH with gusts of 25 to 30 MPH will be \npossible during the mid morning hours through the early afternoon \nhours. The winds will diminish through the afternoon hours on\nThursday as the surface low shifts farther east across central IL.\n\nTuesday looks to be coldest day, as the center of the surface ridge \nacross SD, builds southeast into IA and MO. Highs on Tuesday will \nonly reach the upper 30s north to lower 40s south. Otherwise expect \nhighs in the lower to mid 40s. On Thanksgiving Day, highs will reach \nthe mid to upper 40s.\n\nFriday through Monday:\n\nThe ECMWF is forecasting An H5 trough moving onshore across the \nPacific northwest, then amplifying as it digs southeast across the \nPlains on Saturday. The ascent ahead of the H5 trough combined with \nisentropic lift will provide a chance of rain Saturday afternoon and\nevening. The GFS and Canadian are not as amplified with the H5 \ntrough and show no QPF across the CWA. I'm not really confident in\nrain chances at this time on Saturday afternoon/evening. Once the H5\ntrough shifts east of the area on Sunday, the mid level flow will \nbecome zonal with drier conditions. Highs will reach into the mid to \nupper 50s.",
